<span class="ltx_personname">Haochen You<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Columbia University</span>, <span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_city">New York</span>, <span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">USA</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:hy2854@columbia.edu">hy2854@columbia.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Baojing Liu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Hebei Institute of Communications</span>, <span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_city">Shijiazhuang</span>, <span id="id7" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">China</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">email: </span><a href="mailto:liubj@hebic.edu.cn">liubj@hebic.edu.cn</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2025</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient clipping is widely used to stabilize deep network training, but its formulation as a hard, fixed threshold limits flexibility and ignores gradient distribution dynamics. We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAMP</span> (Statistical Per-layer Adaptive Modulation and Projection), a unified framework that generalizes clipping into smooth, per-layer gradient shaping. SPAMP tracks local gradient statistics, dynamically estimates thresholds, and applies power-based transformations to modulate update magnitudes in a differentiable manner. This perspective recasts clipping and warmup as dual mechanisms for controlling the effective update scale <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, offering a principled alternative to rigid heuristics. Extensive experiments across image and language tasks demonstrate that SPAMP improves stability, convergence, and robustness over existing methods.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Gradient Clipping, Adaptive Optimization, Gradient Norm Shaping, Learning Rate Warmup.
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training deep neural networks efficiently and reliably hinges on effective gradient-based optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. At the heart of this process lies a delicate balance between fast descent and numerical stability-one that depends not only on the learning rate but also on the magnitude of gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koloskova et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee, 2024</a>)</cite>. While the learning rate <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> has been extensively studied and finely tuned via schedules and adaptive methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gradient norm</span> <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is often treated as a passive quantity-measured, monitored, and occasionally bounded via ad-hoc clipping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tucat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient clipping, particularly global norm clipping, has emerged as a popular technique to prevent catastrophic updates, especially in the early stages of training when gradients can be volatile <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. A commonly adopted default in practice is to clip gradients to a maximum <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shulgin and Richtárik, 2024</a>)</cite>. This heuristic, though empirically effective, raises a series of natural questions: Why is <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> used so broadly? Is it merely a conservative bound, or does it reflect a deeper statistical regularity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allouah et al., 2025</a>)</cite>? More importantly, should <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> remain fixed, or can it be adapted-even learned-as part of the training process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu, 2016</a>)</cite>?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work begins with these questions. Drawing on observations from large-scale training and prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we argue that gradient clipping is more than a fail-safe-it acts as a central controller of update magnitude, tightly coupled with learning rate dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koloskova et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Specifically, we show that clipping regulates the product <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}|g_{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}|g_{t}|</annotation></semantics></math>, which is critical to both descent speed and stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tucat et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. This reveals an underappreciated duality: warmup controls <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}|g_{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}|g_{t}|</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, while clipping controls it via <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|g_{t}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g_{t}|</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Together, they form an implicit update magnitude scheduler.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, traditional clipping suffers from several limitations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. It applies a hard thresholding rule with no awareness of layer-wise variance, ignores the distributional structure of gradients, and introduces non-differentiable discontinuities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mai and Johansson, 2021</a>)</cite>. These drawbacks motivate a shift from fixed-threshold clipping to a smoother, functional, and statistically grounded alternative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAMP</span>, a unified framework for gradient norm shaping. Our contributions are as follows: (1) We reformulate gradient clipping as a smooth, differentiable operator and generalize it into a family of gradient shaping functions that unify warmup, norm clipping, and gradient normalization; (2) We design SPAMP, which combines per-layer statistical tracking with power-based modulation to adaptively control gradient scales; (3) We provide theoretical insights into how SPAMP shapes loss descent dynamics and regulates update magnitudes across layers and time; (4) We empirically demonstrate that SPAMP improves convergence speed, robustness, and final performance on image classification and transformer-based models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the standard supervised learning setup, where the goal is to minimize a loss function <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> over parameters <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, typically via stochastic gradient-based optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote model parameters at step <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}:=\nabla\mathcal{L}_{t}(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}:=\nabla\mathcal{L}_{t}(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> the stochastic gradient computed on a mini-batch. The standard update rule is <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}=\theta_{t}-\eta_{t}g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}=\theta_{t}-\eta_{t}g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. While practical optimizers (e.g., Adam, Momentum SGD) may include additional dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba, 2014</a>)</cite>, we focus on this canonical form unless otherwise noted.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To stabilize training under large gradients, global gradient clipping is often applied <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koloskova et al., 2023</a>)</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
\frac{\tau}{\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau\end{cases}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
\frac{\tau}{\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed clipping threshold, often empirically set to 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth loss functions, a first-order approximation gives the per-step descent:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}:=\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t+1})-\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\approx-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}:=\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t+1})-\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\approx-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates controlling the update magnitude <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure safe and effective descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tucat et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, especially during early training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout our analysis, we assume:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Smoothness:</span> <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth: <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{1})-\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{2})\|\leq L\|\theta_{1}-\theta_{2}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{1})-\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{2})\|\leq L\|\theta_{1}-\theta_{2}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded variance:</span> <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbiased with <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|g_{t}-\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}]\leq\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|g_{t}-\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}]\leq\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non-degeneracy:</span> <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>From Clipping to Gradient Shaping: A Functional Perspective on Update Control</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>The Active Role of <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> in Gradient Descent</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient clipping is traditionally regarded as a reactive safety device, suppressing occasional gradient explosions. However, recent theoretical and empirical analyses suggest that the clipping threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> actively shapes the descent dynamics by modulating the effective update scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hübler et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In this section, we formalize how <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the learning rate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gradient norm <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and argue that it implicitly defines a ceiling on the per-step loss reduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assuming <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth, a first-order Taylor approximation yields:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t+1})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})-\eta_{t}\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}+\frac{L}{2}\eta_{t}^{2}\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t+1})\leq\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})-\eta_{t}\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}+\frac{L}{2}\eta_{t}^{2}\|\nabla\mathcal{L}(\theta_{t})\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Neglecting the second-order term and substituting a stochastic gradient <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected descent becomes approximately <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}\approx-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}\approx-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approximation makes clear that the product <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the rate of descent, but also introduces a stability risk when <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is large-a common occurrence in early training, due to random initialization and uncalibrated activations. If <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is not carefully attenuated, the resulting large step may overshoot, diverge, or destabilize the learning trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Gradient clipping modifies <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by enforcing <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, replacing it with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}=(\tau/\|g_{t}\|)g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}=(\tau/\|g_{t}\|)g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> when necessary. This imposes an upper bound on the effective update norm. Substituting <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> into the descent estimate gives:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}\approx\begin{cases}-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2},&amp;\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
-\eta_{t}\tau^{2},&amp;\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}\approx\begin{cases}-\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|^{2},&amp;\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
-\eta_{t}\tau^{2},&amp;\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> controls the maximum per-step reduction in loss, transforming clipping from a passive failsafe into a dynamic descent-rate governor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gorbunov et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. It follows that the choice of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> critically influences optimization speed and stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To understand suitable values of <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, consider that <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> often follows a sub-exponential or heavy-tailed distribution. If we model <math id="S3.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}\|&gt;x)\leq Ce^{-\lambda x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}\|&gt;x)\leq Ce^{-\lambda x}</annotation></semantics></math>, the expected clipped descent becomes:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}]=-\eta_{t}\left(\mathbb{E}[\|g_{t}\|^{2}\cdot\mathbb{I}_{\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau}]+\tau^{2}\cdot\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\Delta\mathcal{L}_{t}]=-\eta_{t}\left(\mathbb{E}[\|g_{t}\|^{2}\cdot\mathbb{I}_{\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau}]+\tau^{2}\cdot\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This expression exhibits a natural trade-off: smaller <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> leads to safer but slower updates; larger <math id="S3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> allows faster descent but increases the risk of instability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, the mode or median of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> often lies near 1, which explains why <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> performs well across many architectures. But this success reflects statistical regularity, not optimality: when the distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> shifts-across layers, optimizers, or tasks-the fixed threshold becomes suboptimal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gurbuzbalaban et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In sum, the clipping threshold <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is not merely a stability safeguard, but a key factor in governing optimization dynamics. By bounding <math id="S3.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, it implicitly defines the largest allowable descent, and thus participates in regulating both convergence and robustness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Empirical Origins of <math id="S3.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> and Limitations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common empirical heuristic in large-scale model training is to set the global gradient clipping threshold to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This value appears frequently across implementations and has demonstrated robustness across model families and tasks. However, its effectiveness is not a result of universal optimality, but of a consistent statistical structure observed in the distribution of gradient norms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the stochastic gradient at step <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> with norm <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. Empirical observations across various architectures show that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> typically concentrates in a narrow band, especially after the early warmup phase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schaipp et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The probability density <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> often peaks near <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math>, with negligible mass for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\gg 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\gg 2</annotation></semantics></math>. In cumulative terms, the empirical CDF <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}(r)</annotation></semantics></math> typically satisfies <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}(1)\approx 0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}(1)\approx 0.8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{t}(2)\approx 0.98" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.98</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{t}(2)\approx 0.98</annotation></semantics></math>, implying that a threshold of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> clips only a small minority (top <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math>) of updates, while preserving most gradients untouched.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This makes <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> act effectively as a soft quantile-based filter-a robust central tendency aligned with the distributional mode or median of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, one could generalize this by letting <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}:=\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}:=\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the median of the gradient norm distribution at step <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Such a formulation adapts <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> dynamically to the empirical geometry of the gradient landscape.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">That said, the statistical validity of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> is inherently conditional. It assumes the underlying distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is stable and unimodal-an assumption that often fails in deeper models, across layers, or under curriculum learning, optimizer transitions, or batch-size scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Furthermore, global statistics may obscure layer-wise disparities, as later sections will show. In such cases, a fixed <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> may either overclip critical signals or allow harmful outliers to pass unchecked.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Hence, while the prevalence of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> is grounded in real statistical regularities, it should not be misinterpreted as a structural optimum. Rather, it serves as a practical proxy for a deeper principle: that clipping thresholds should track the empirical center of gradient norm distributions, adjusting as those distributions shift. This motivates the design of dynamic, context-sensitive mechanisms-a direction we pursue next.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Adapting <math id="S3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> via Layer-wise Gradient Statistics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> works well empirically, fixed global thresholds fundamentally fail to accommodate the heterogeneity and dynamics of gradient norms encountered in large-scale training. Gradients vary significantly across layers, training phases, and optimizer states-and their distributions often exhibit long tails rather than Gaussian concentration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In transformer-style networks, for instance, gradients at different layers can differ in scale by more than an order of magnitude. Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the gradient at layer <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and step <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>; empirical observations show that <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{l}\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|/\min_{l}\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|\gg 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{l}\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|/\min_{l}\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|\gg 10</annotation></semantics></math>. A single global <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> then simultaneously overclips large layers and underclips small ones, disrupting both stability and coordination. Moreover, the distribution of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|</annotation></semantics></math> often exhibits heavy-tailed behavior-closer to log-normal or Pareto than to Gaussian-with <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|&gt;r)\propto r^{-\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|&gt;r)\propto r^{-\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. In such regimes, clipping with a fixed cutoff either suppresses too much signal or lets outliers destabilize learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These issues motivate adaptive thresholding. A natural strategy is to define <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> via exponential moving averages:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}^{(l)}:=\beta\cdot\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}+(1-\beta)\cdot\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}^{(l)}:=\beta\cdot\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}+(1-\beta)\cdot\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0.9,0.999]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0.9,0.999]</annotation></semantics></math> controls smoothness. Clipping is then applied per-layer via</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}=\frac{\tau_{t}^{(l)}}{\max(\tau_{t}^{(l)},\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|)}\cdot g_{t}^{(l)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}=\frac{\tau_{t}^{(l)}}{\max(\tau_{t}^{(l)},\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|)}\cdot g_{t}^{(l)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This mechanism tracks the central tendency of <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|</annotation></semantics></math> in real time, suppresses transient spikes, and eliminates the need to hand-tune global constants. As shown in AdaGC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, such dynamic per-layer clipping improves training stability, especially in the early phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">From a theoretical standpoint, this mechanism can be viewed as implementing a bound on the update norm: for a desired upper limit <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we implicitly enforce <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\cdot\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\cdot\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> tracks the mean or median of <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and assuming bounded second moments, it follows that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\eta_{t}\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|^{2}]\leq\eta_{t}^{2}\cdot\mathbb{E}[(\tau_{t}^{(l)})^{2}]\leq\delta^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\eta_{t}\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|^{2}]\leq\eta_{t}^{2}\cdot\mathbb{E}[(\tau_{t}^{(l)})^{2}]\leq\delta^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This formulation reframes clipping as a form of norm-based update scheduling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, aligning its role with warmup, normalization, and learning-rate scaling.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In short, fixed thresholds ignore both the variability and statistical geometry of gradient norms. Modeling <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> as a low-variance estimator of recent gradient behavior-rather than as a static scalar-yields more flexible, robust, and interpretable control over update magnitudes. This perspective supports a shift from global safeguards to local, data-driven shaping of the training trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Unifying Warmup and Clipping through Update Magnitude Control</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread use of warmup schedules-where the learning rate <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> starts from a small value and increases gradually-is often justified heuristically as “starting slow.” However, a more precise interpretation is that warmup regulates the effective update magnitude <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\cdot\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\cdot\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, which governs the size of parameter changes per step <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. This connects directly to gradient clipping, which constrains <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and reveals a shared objective: to stabilize training by bounding update norms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, consider the update <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}=\theta_{t}-\eta_{t}g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}=\theta_{t}-\eta_{t}g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and define <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}:=\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}:=\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. Training stability requires that <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> remains below a threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is large-as is common in early training-then even moderate values of <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can cause explosive updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. Warmup schedules mitigate this by slowly increasing <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively enforcing an inverse relation <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\leq\delta/\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\leq\delta/\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This same quantity <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> is also bounded when gradient clipping is applied. When <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, clipping enforces <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{g}_{t}\|=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{g}_{t}\|=\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the update norm becomes <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\tau</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, warmup and clipping provide complementary pathways for regulating <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>: either adapt <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> (as in GradNorm), or constrain <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These observations motivate a unified formulation. Define a rule:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|\leq\delta\\
\frac{\delta}{\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\text{if }\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|\leq\delta\\
\frac{\delta}{\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This “update clipping” directly enforces <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\eta_{t}\tilde{g}_{t}\|\leq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\eta_{t}\tilde{g}_{t}\|\leq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of the values of <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> individually. Unlike warmup or standard clipping, which target only one side of the product <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\cdot\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\cdot\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, this approach modulates their interaction explicitly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> itself can be adapted to training dynamics. Let <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{t}:=\mathrm{EMA}_{\beta}(\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|)+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>EMA</mi><mi>β</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{t}:=\mathrm{EMA}_{\beta}(\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|)+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0.9,0.999]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0.9,0.999]</annotation></semantics></math>. This tracks the running update magnitude and adjusts the bound, generalizing warmup into a continual norm-aware schedule.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This perspective unifies disparate techniques-warmup schedules, gradient clipping, and GradNorm-style inverse scaling-under the single objective of bounding the update magnitude <math id="S3.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>. It also clarifies that what matters for stability is not just the learning rate or gradient norm in isolation, but their joint product, which can be directly controlled via smooth, adaptive mechanisms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Generalizing Clipping via Smooth Gradient Shaping Operators</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Previous sections treated <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as a scalar-fixed or dynamically estimated-governing a binary clipping rule. Yet this view still frames gradient regulation as a thresholding operation: if the norm exceeds <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, rescale; otherwise, pass unchanged. In contrast, we suggest viewing clipping, normalization, and warmup not as separate heuristics, but as instances of a broader class of <span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gradient shaping functions</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Classical clipping imposes a hard discontinuity:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
\frac{\tau}{\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}=\begin{cases}g_{t},&amp;\|g_{t}\|\leq\tau\\
\frac{\tau}{\|g_{t}\|}g_{t},&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This transformation is non-differentiable at <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|=\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|=\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, suppresses large gradients entirely, and may distort optimization trajectories when invoked frequently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these limitations, we consider continuous, differentiable shaping functions <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:\mathbb{R}^{d}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which smoothly transform the gradient <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}=S(g_{t};\theta_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}=S(g_{t};\theta_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Examples include power-based shaping functions, where each coordinate is transformed as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t,i}=\mathrm{sign}(g_{t,i})\cdot|g_{t,i}|^{\alpha}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t,i}=\mathrm{sign}(g_{t,i})\cdot|g_{t,i}|^{\alpha},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> compressing large magnitudes (soft clipping), and <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> amplifying them (aggressive descent). The effective norm becomes <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{g}_{t}\|=\left(\sum_{i}|g_{t,i}|^{2\alpha}\right)^{1/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{g}_{t}\|=\left(\sum_{i}|g_{t,i}|^{2\alpha}\right)^{1/2}</annotation></semantics></math>, offering a continuous analog to norm constraint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">More generally, the exponent <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> can be made dynamic, e.g., <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}=h(\|g_{t}\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}=h(\|g_{t}\|)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> decreasing, to induce magnitude-sensitive softening. This transforms <math id="S3.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> from a scalar bound into an implicit controller of shaping curvature-adapting not just when gradients are large, but how aggressively they are modified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This functional viewpoint subsumes multiple strategies:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clipping: <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(g)=\frac{\tau}{\|g\|}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(g)=\frac{\tau}{\|g\|}g</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g\|&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g\|&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Warmup: <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(g)=\eta_{t}g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(g)=\eta_{t}g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> increasing over time</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Power transformation: <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(g_{i})=\mathrm{sign}(g_{i})\cdot|g_{i}|^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(g_{i})=\mathrm{sign}(g_{i})\cdot|g_{i}|^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Normalization: <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(g)=g/\|g\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(g)=g/\|g\|</annotation></semantics></math> (unit direction updates)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Rather than selecting one mechanism, this formulation allows shaping operators to be composed, scheduled, or even learned, forming a <span id="S3.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gradient modulation pipeline</span> that flexibly controls both the magnitude and direction of updates across training time and network depth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p">By functionalizing <math id="S3.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we transition from fixed-threshold clipping to a general framework of smooth, differentiable, and context-aware shaping. This reframing completes the theoretical arc from empirical heuristics to structured regulation mechanisms, setting the stage for concrete algorithmic realizations. We summarize the core logic of SPAMP as a unified update rule that combines dynamic clipping, gradient shaping, and per-layer statistics in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 3.5. Generalizing Clipping via Smooth Gradient Shaping Operators ‣ 3. From Clipping to Gradient Shaping: A Functional Perspective on Update Control ‣ Gradient Shaping Beyond Clipping: A Functional Perspective on Update Magnitude Control" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> SPAMP Update at Step <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Gradient <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rate <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, previous thresholds <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, smoothing <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, shaping function <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each layer <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
              Estimate dynamic norm target: <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow\beta\cdot\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}+(1-\beta)\cdot\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow\beta\cdot\tau_{t-1}^{(l)}+(1-\beta)\cdot\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              Compute shaping exponent: <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow h(\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|/\tau_{t}^{(l)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow h(\|g_{t}^{(l)}\|/\tau_{t}^{(l)})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              Apply shaping: <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow\operatorname{sign}(g_{t}^{(l)})\cdot|g_{t}^{(l)}|^{\alpha_{t}^{(l)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow\operatorname{sign}(g_{t}^{(l)})\cdot|g_{t}^{(l)}|^{\alpha_{t}^{(l)}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|&gt;\tau_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|&gt;\tau_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               Rescale: <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow(\tau_{t}^{(l)}/\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|)\cdot\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\leftarrow(\tau_{t}^{(l)}/\|\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}\|)\cdot\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              Update: <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}^{(l)}\leftarrow\theta_{t}^{(l)}-\eta_{t}\cdot\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}^{(l)}\leftarrow\theta_{t}^{(l)}-\eta_{t}\cdot\tilde{g}_{t}^{(l)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments aim to evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed framework in terms of training stability, convergence speed, and final model performance. We assess whether controlling the update magnitude <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> via dynamic shaping yields improvements over traditional clipping and warmup strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compared Methods.</span> We compare the following optimization variants:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline (SGD / Adam)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba, 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Robbins and Monro, 1951</a>)</cite>: No clipping, no warmup.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fixed Clipping</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2019</a>)</cite>: Global norm clipping with a fixed threshold <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warmup + Clipping</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kosson et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: Linear warmup for <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> combined with fixed <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradNorm</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2018</a>)</cite>: Learning rate scaled inversely with <math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ZClip</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Gradient clipping based on z-score anomalies with EMA statistics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPAM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: Spike-aware Adam optimizer with momentum reset and clipping.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">All methods use identical initialization and learning rate schedules unless explicitly modified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and Datasets.</span> We consider both vision and language benchmarks across model scales, including <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">image classification</span> with ResNet-18 on CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Krizhevsky et al., 2009</a>)</cite>, <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">text classification</span> with a Transformer encoder on SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Socher et al., 2013</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">language modeling</span> with a 12-layer GPT-style decoder on WikiText-103 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Merity et al., 2016</a>)</cite> (truncated subset). This selection ensures coverage of shallow vs. deep, convolutional vs. attention-based, and small vs. medium-scale regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Training Details.</span> All models are trained with batch size 128, initial learning rate 0.001 (for Adam), and a cosine decay schedule. For our method, dynamic <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated via EMA with <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, and power shaping uses <math id="S4.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}\in[0.7,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}\in[0.7,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math> based on normalized gradient statistics. We train each configuration for 100 epochs (or 50k steps for language models), and repeat experiments with 3 different seeds to report average and variance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span> We track and compare three key metrics: <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">convergence speed</span> (measured by the number of steps required to reach a fixed loss or accuracy threshold), <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">final performance</span> (quantified by accuracy for classification tasks or perplexity for language modeling), and <span id="S4.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">training stability</span> (assessed via gradient variance, update magnitude <math id="S4.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and clipping frequency).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Convergence Dynamics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate optimization efficiency and stability, we track training loss and validation performance over time on CIFAR-10 (ResNet-18) and WikiText-103 (GPT-style decoder). Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2. Convergence Dynamics ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Gradient Shaping Beyond Clipping: A Functional Perspective on Update Magnitude Control" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that our method converges faster and more smoothly than fixed-threshold or warmup-based strategies. Baselines without clipping suffer from spikes and stagnation, while ZClip and SPAM reduce some instability but lag in final loss. In contrast, dynamic shaping yields steady descent and accelerated early-stage progress.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01578v1/loss_curves_with_bold_variation.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/150;" width="467" height="150"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Training loss vs steps on CIFAR-10 and WikiText-103 for various optimization strategies. Our method shows smooth, accelerated convergence.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Final validation accuracy (CIFAR-10, SST-2) and perplexity (WikiText-103) are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2. Convergence Dynamics ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Gradient Shaping Beyond Clipping: A Functional Perspective on Update Magnitude Control" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Our method achieves the best overall performance, with ZClip and SPAM partially closing the gap but showing instability or slower starts. GradNorm helps early on but plateaus prematurely. Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.2. Convergence Dynamics ‣ 4. Experiments ‣ Gradient Shaping Beyond Clipping: A Functional Perspective on Update Magnitude Control" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents smoothed update magnitudes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}\|g_{t}\|</annotation></semantics></math> over time, where our method maintains consistently bounded and stable updates-supporting the view that update magnitude is a central stability regulator.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">CIFAR-10 Acc</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">SST-2 Acc</span></th>
<th id="S4.T1.3.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T1.3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">WikiText-103 PPL</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Baseline</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">85.1 <math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.7</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">86.2 <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.9</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.3.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">41.2 <math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 1.3</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fixed Clipping</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">88.3 <math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.5</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">88.7 <math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.3.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">35.5 <math id="S4.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 1.0</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Warmup + Clip</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">89.1 <math id="S4.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.4</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">89.4 <math id="S4.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.5</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.3.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T1.3.4.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">33.9 <math id="S4.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.9</span></td></tr>
